-- title:
--   §3.1, equations (6)–(7): faithful/direct-sum reduction
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   In an admissible structural presentation, the representation kernel $\mathfrak l_0\trianglelefteq\mathfrak l$ has a complementary ideal $\mathfrak l_1$. The resulting subspaces are ideals and give
--
--   $$\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g_1\oplus\mathfrak l_0,\qquad\mathfrak g_1=\mathfrak z\oplus V\oplus\mathfrak l_1.$$
--
--   The kernel factor is reductive and $\mathfrak l_1$ acts faithfully on $V$. For $x=x_1+x_0$ in this decomposition, $\operatorname{co}(x)$ is the product of the intrinsic factor orbit hulls; consequently $\mathfrak g_{co}=\mathfrak g_{1,co}\times\mathfrak l_{0,co}$. The Lean statement expresses the product via the sum map and proves kernel/complement existence rather than assuming factorization.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 7, §3.1, equations (6)–(7): faithful/direct-sum reduction

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Frontier30_P26

